      "n": 1,
      "title": "Geologic Time Scale & History of Life",
      "competency": "STEM_BIO11/12-IIIC-g-8",
      "content": "<h3>🎯 Learning Objectives</h3><ul><li>Describe the geologic time scale.</li><li>Identify major eras and periods.</li><li>Explain the sequence of major life events.</li><li>Relate geologic events to biological evolution.</li></ul><h3>🔑 Key Concepts</h3><ul><li><strong>Geologic time scale</strong> — timeline of Earth's history.</li><li><strong>Eons, eras, periods, epochs</strong> — divisions of time.</li><li><strong>Mass extinctions</strong> — events that wiped out many species.</li></ul><h3>📚 Lesson Content</h3><h4>A. Major Divisions</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Era</th><th>Time</th><th>Key Events</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Precambrian</td><td>4.6B – 541M years ago</td><td>First life, first cells, oxygen atmosphere</td></tr><tr><td>Paleozoic</td><td>541–252M years ago</td><td>Cambrian explosion, first fish, plants, amphibians</td></tr><tr><td>Mesozoic</td><td>252–66M years ago</td><td>Age of reptiles, dinosaurs, first birds and mammals</td></tr><tr><td>Cenozoic</td><td>66M years ago – present</td><td>Age of mammals, first humans</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>B. Mass Extinctions</h4><ul><li>End-Permian (~252 MYA) — 96% of species lost</li><li>End-Cretaceous (~66 MYA) — dinosaurs extinct</li></ul>",
      "activity1": {
